questions.answer:
A function f is continuous at x = a if and only if all three of the following hold:
(i) f(a) is defined, i.e. a lies in the domain of f;
(ii) lim (x -> a) f(x) exists, i.e. the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal;
(iii) the limit equals the function value: lim (x -> a) f(x) = f(a).
If any one condition fails, f is discontinuous at a.
